00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:16.090
And how did you get
involved with unikernels?

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:18.120
Could you tell us a
little bit about the story

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:22.500
of how you doing these
kind of cloud software

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:25.700
ends up doing a talk about
unikernels at Container Camp?

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:27.380
JOHN FEMINELLA: Sure.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:29.600
You'll often see me
giving talks about things

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:32.830
that I have basically
no affiliation with,

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:36.160
but I find incredibly
interesting in some way.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.990
And unikernels is one
of the topics that's

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:41.580
very squarely in that category.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:46.460
So I certainly haven't
done any academic research

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:48.730
in the unikernel space,
which is a lot of where

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:51.530
the genesis of
where we're seeing

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:53.830
the interest in
unikernels come from.

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:57.460
It was very much an
academic idea to start with,

00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:59.810
that kind of got
gradually evolved

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:05.260
into an industry exploration
that's now going on.

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:11.430
So there haven't been
many practical examples

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:14.300
of unikernels until, I would
say, relatively recently

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:15.970
in computing history.

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:21.220
And that sort of is what drew
my attention to it first,

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.210
was the academic
side of the thing.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:26.370
In January 2014, there
was a publication

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:29.510
made in a magazine called
"Communications of the ACM,"

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:33.870
and "CACM" is sort
of a-- it's halfway

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:38.310
between a formal journal
and, like, a gossip

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:40.105
magazine for
computer scientists.

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.365
I hope there are no "CACM"
board members on here,

00:07:45.365 --> 00:07:47.490
because they would be very
mad at that description.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.510
But it's basically a
very-- it's kind of a way

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:52.560
to get really interesting
ideas out in front

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:55.940
of a large audience without
necessarily requiring

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:59.140
the rigor of a
formal academic paper

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:02.150
that you might submit
to a larger journal.

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:04.020
And I love "CACM"
for that reason,

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:06.860
because it's a great
bridge from academia,

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:09.540
to kind of describe what they're
working on, and for industry

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:11.706
folks to kind of hear about
what's happening, and up

00:08:11.706 --> 00:08:12.290
and coming.

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:13.910
And so there was
a paper published

00:08:13.910 --> 00:08:16.180
in January 2014
called "Unikernels:

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:20.090
The Rise of the Virtual
Library Operating System,"

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:24.751
written by-- David J. Scott
is one of the authors,

00:08:24.751 --> 00:08:26.750
and I'm probably going
to mispronounce his name,

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:30.920
but I think the other
author is Anil Madhavapeddy.

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:35.059
And both of them were
talking about how

00:08:35.059 --> 00:08:40.940
this new way of consolidating
machine images and computing

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:44.720
resources into sort of
this discrete single thing,

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:48.700
in a very specific tailored way,
could yield enormous benefits.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:50.580
And that was very
interesting to me,

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:53.020
because that's almost
the same claim that

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:56.950
was made for the
advantages for containers.

00:08:56.950 --> 00:09:01.384
And when containers were
announced-- or not announced--

00:09:01.384 --> 00:09:03.050
but when containers
were being developed

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:05.650
over the last decade,
or couple of decades,

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:08.890
as a technology, that
was one of the claims

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:11.910
that the proponents of
containers often pointed to,

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:14.440
was, like, there's
these sort of advantages

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.810
to consolidating and
minimizing the surface

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:20.220
area of what you're actually
delivering to a production

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:21.190
system.

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:23.060
And unikernels seems
to be something

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:25.920
of an extension of that idea,
at least on the surface.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.470
And that's very
interesting to me,

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:31.474
because, as someone who works
a lot with cloud computing,

00:09:31.474 --> 00:09:32.890
and working with
these enterprises

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:35.760
who deliver software
in the cloud,

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:38.070
I'm very interested
in what the correct,

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:42.480
or what the interesting new,
ways of deploying software

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:47.070
in large scale environments,
in a reliable, consistent, and

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.942
repeatable way,
what that looks like

00:09:49.942 --> 00:09:51.150
through different strategies.

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:52.990
And unikernels
definitely represents

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:56.140
a new point in the
spectrum that I don't think

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:57.410
has really existed before.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:09:59.790
So it's a new sort of
tool in our toolbox

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:01.704
that's evolving over time.

00:10:01.704 --> 00:10:02.620
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:07.250
So before we start talking about
the advantages or disadvantages

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:09.460
of unikernels, why
don't you tell us

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:12.620
a little bit about what is
a unikernel specifically,

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:14.600
from the point of
view of someone

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:18.225
that, like me, knows Docker,
but that's pretty much it?

00:10:18.225 --> 00:10:19.100
JOHN FEMINELLA: Sure.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:21.880
So if you know about
Docker, you probably

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:25.340
know that the way that Docker
is able to deliver some

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:28.840
of the isolation
advantages that it has

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:32.680
is because there are specific
hooks inside of your kernel

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:38.360
that allow a machine that's
running Docker to guarantee

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:40.030
those isolation properties.

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:42.750
So your kernel is, in
some sense, aware of this,

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:45.360
and it's providing
those benefits for you.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:49.250
In older, more traditional
style isolation

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:52.880
through virtual machines, the
way that you get isolation

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.030
is by running an
entire new OS stack

00:10:56.030 --> 00:11:00.540
on top of your existing
OS and existing services

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:01.200
and libraries.

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:05.090
So you're basically repeating
all the work of building an OS.

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:08.670
And with something like Docker,
or another containerization

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:11.540
technology, you're
removing the need

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:14.340
to have kind of a big
heavy kernel in there,

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:16.700
and you kind of just
have lightweight hooks

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:19.630
into a real running
kernel, and then

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:23.220
you get performance
advantages by not

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:25.060
having to virtualize
those calls across

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:27.870
as many layers as a
virtual machine has to.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:29.960
And unikernels go
one step farther.

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:31.930
And unikernels are
based on the observation

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:35.680
that, if you have
a kernel and you

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:39.290
have an OS in the form
of additional services

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:41.110
and libraries on top
of the kernel that

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:44.210
are made available to
applications-- if you have

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:49.180
that-- you pretty quickly reach
the conclusion that, well,

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:52.510
not every application
running on top of that stack

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:55.240
needs everything
that's in the whole OS,

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:56.730
and in the whole kernel.

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:57.230
Right?

00:11:57.230 --> 00:12:03.032
So your web browser probably
needs a lot of the kernel,

00:12:03.032 --> 00:12:04.490
but it probably
doesn't necessarily

00:12:04.490 --> 00:12:13.330
need things like access to
your serial port, or whatever.

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:15.024
And that's true for
many different kinds

00:12:15.024 --> 00:12:15.690
of applications.

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:17.840
They don't need access
to the entire kernel.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:20.230
So unikernels are a
way of saying, well,

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:23.890
what if we didn't need to
have the whole kernel around,

00:12:23.890 --> 00:12:26.690
and the whole OS around,
for an application?

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:28.440
What if we could just
take the pieces that

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:31.680
are relevant to an application?

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:33.280
And what if we could
run it that way?

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:35.690
What if we could
sort of only expose,

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:38.990
or make available, to
the application the parts

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:40.625
that it really depends on?

00:12:40.625 --> 00:12:42.510
And if we can do
that, then maybe we

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:44.840
can ship that entire
dependency stack

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:47.530
as a single, consolidated
machine image.

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:52.540
And then, just like we can run
Docker containers containing

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:55.710
images, we should be able
to run sort of a container

00:12:55.710 --> 00:12:58.060
around this consolidated
machine image.

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:02.890
But because that image is
effectively an entire operating

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:04.910
system in a bottle,
you could run that

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:09.530
on bare metal, or on a
hypervisor, or whatever.

00:13:09.530 --> 00:13:14.060
And also, because
it's so much smaller,

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:16.559
we expect to see
certain benefits around.

00:13:16.559 --> 00:13:18.600
Like, maybe it's quicker
to start those things up

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:21.890
than it would be to start a
container or a virtual machine,

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:22.830
and so on.

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:25.540
So once you start
thinking about how can we

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:28.660
shrink the set of
stuff you actually

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:32.770
need-- only the
dependencies required

00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:36.300
by a process or an application--
it starts really motivating

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:38.430
some interesting thought
patterns around how we're

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:40.710
going to design those
applications, what kinds

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:43.280
of applications are really
well suited for this,

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:44.950
and which ones
aren't, and so on.

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:46.700
So it really opens
up a new space

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:49.270
to think about how to
deliver and deploy,

00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:51.300
and maybe even design,
our applications--

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:52.841
to take advantage of that.

00:13:52.841 --> 00:13:53.590
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:58.400
So that's really interesting,
but from a practical point

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:00.960
of view, how do I,
like, run a unikernel?

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:02.560
How do I build a unikernel?

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:03.714
Is there tooling available?

00:14:03.714 --> 00:14:05.880
Like, I'm obviously familiar
with sort or containers

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:07.546
that can sort of put
those in the cloud.

00:14:07.546 --> 00:14:10.410
But do I need to run each
on an individual machine?

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:11.400
How does that work?

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:12.275
JOHN FEMINELLA: Sure.

00:14:14.504 --> 00:14:16.670
Again, this is still in
very much an evolving space,

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:19.430
but there are many
different-- right now,

00:14:19.430 --> 00:14:23.900
we're sort of in this
meritocracy phase, where

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:26.150
everyone's trying to do a
bunch of different projects,

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:27.035
and we're trying to
figure out, like,

00:14:27.035 --> 00:14:28.243
what are the best approaches?

00:14:28.243 --> 00:14:31.010
And that's still very
much an evolving idea.

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:34.160
So there have been
a couple of attempts

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:37.486
to consolidate these
ideas into one location

00:14:37.486 --> 00:14:39.110
where people can play
around with them,

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:41.570
but there hasn't been much
consolidation that I've

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:43.210
seen in the way of projects.

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:45.520
So people are still kind
of independently exploring

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:46.860
many different ideas.

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:49.160
And to get a taste of
the many different ways

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:51.250
that we get started
with unikernels,

00:14:51.250 --> 00:14:53.610
I would recommend
that folks check out

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:56.230
a website called unikernel.org.

00:14:56.230 --> 00:14:59.730
And this is the work
of a couple of people,

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:04.030
including Amir Chaudhry, who was
formerly at Unikernel Systems

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:06.520
and is now with Docker.

00:15:06.520 --> 00:15:08.430
And he's done a
great job of kind

00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:12.040
of collecting these different
projects into a single place,

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:15.620
and giving you a
really broad exposure

00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:17.880
to the many different
kinds of ways

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:19.920
that you could actually
build a unikernel.

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:21.800
I will say that,
broadly speaking,

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:24.380
there are two basic strategies.

00:15:24.380 --> 00:15:32.640
You either start with an
application that exists,

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:35.580
or you're willing to start from
scratch to build some service,

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:36.300
or whatever.

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:38.091
If you're starting from
an application that

00:15:38.091 --> 00:15:42.130
already exists, you probably
have a POSIX compatible

00:15:42.130 --> 00:15:43.180
application.

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:46.870
And if that's true, then
you can use something

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:54.210
called Rumprun, which is a way
of taking your-- basically,

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:57.080
a rump kernel is a special
flavor of unikernel,

00:15:57.080 --> 00:16:00.110
where basically you can
build the software stack

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:03.000
you need for your application
without having to build all

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:04.770
the individual pieces yourself.

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:10.210
So for example, if you don't
want all the overhead of an OS,

00:16:10.210 --> 00:16:13.340
you still may need drivers
to actually run that OS.

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:16.020
So for example, your
web application probably

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:20.400
needs access to TCP/IP, protocol
stacks, and things like that.

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:23.995
So you still need these pieces
to run your application,

00:16:23.995 --> 00:16:27.040
and a rump kernel lets
you assemble those pieces

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:29.470
in a way that's POSIX
compliant, but your application

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:31.130
will still probably work.

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:34.154
And so that's one
way of minimizing

00:16:34.154 --> 00:16:36.320
what comes along for the
ride with your application.

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:39.805
So you wind up with
a very compact image

00:16:39.805 --> 00:16:43.010
that then you can run
on top of raw hardware,

00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:45.580
or a cloud hypervisor,
so KVM or Xen.

00:16:45.580 --> 00:16:48.410
You would take this image and
you would run it on top of Xen,

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:49.900
let's say.

00:16:49.900 --> 00:16:51.190
So that's one strategy.

00:16:51.190 --> 00:16:55.264
The second strategy is
you are willing to sort

00:16:55.264 --> 00:16:56.180
of start from scratch.

00:16:56.180 --> 00:16:58.450
So maybe you're just
experimenting, for example.

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:01.710
And there are what I'm
going to call, broadly,

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:04.819
language specific unikernels.

00:17:04.819 --> 00:17:07.430
So there are operating
system stacks

00:17:07.430 --> 00:17:10.030
that require your
application to be written

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:12.369
in a very specific language.

00:17:12.369 --> 00:17:15.970
So for example,
Mirage OS is what

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:20.050
I would say is the canonical
unikernel OS for OCaml,

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:20.950
at this point.

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:23.270
So Mirage OS has a
bunch of libraries.

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:26.160
You have your application.

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:30.650
You say, hey, I need a
networking stack, or whatever,

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:34.190
and the Mirage OS compiler
determines, you know,

00:17:34.190 --> 00:17:37.070
what the set of
libraries you need

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:39.050
is, and then compiles
that into a machine

00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:42.261
image that you can, then, run
just like you did with the rump

00:17:42.261 --> 00:17:42.760
kernel.

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:47.040
You can run that machine
image on top of Xen.

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:50.180
Now, the extent to which
each project supports

00:17:50.180 --> 00:17:53.160
this, or the levels
of documentation

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:57.020
and how easy it is to get
started, vary really wildly.

00:17:57.020 --> 00:18:01.020
I consider myself a
relative OCaml novice,

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:02.650
but I really liked Mirage OS.

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:06.130
I liked getting
started with OCaml,

00:18:06.130 --> 00:18:09.230
and I built a really
small web service

00:18:09.230 --> 00:18:14.540
that talks to a few different--
it's just like a quote service

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:17.730
so you can talk to
Yahoo and get the stock

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:20.045
price of a particular
quote, and things like that.

00:18:20.045 --> 00:18:21.670
But it was really
easy to build, and it

00:18:21.670 --> 00:18:22.711
was very straightforward.

00:18:22.711 --> 00:18:25.240
So I've been liking
Mirage OS the most.

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:27.737
That's not to say that the
others aren't also great.

00:18:27.737 --> 00:18:29.320
But you know, I only
had so much time,

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:31.630
and I don't want to learn,
like, 37 languages to try

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:34.852
37 different flavors
of unikernel OS.

00:18:34.852 --> 00:18:36.560
But that's the basic
idea, is you kind of

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:40.900
have some mechanism that
turns your application

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:45.320
into a compact machine
image, and then you

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:49.050
run that compact machine
image on a hypervisor

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:52.480
of some kind that sits on
top of your bare metal.

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:58.037
And then you're running your
unikernel at that point,

00:18:58.037 --> 00:19:00.120
so then it can receive
requests from the internet,

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:02.150
or write to a
database, or whatever

00:19:02.150 --> 00:19:03.594
else it's supposed to be doing.

00:19:03.594 --> 00:19:04.510
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:19:04.510 --> 00:19:07.367
So if I understand
that correctly,

00:19:07.367 --> 00:19:09.450
does that mean that if I
am running, for instance,

00:19:09.450 --> 00:19:13.240
a web server in, let's
say, Go-- because I

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:17.630
like Go-- I could
eventually-- rather than

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:24.690
building a binary that runs on
Linux-- I could build a Linux,

00:19:24.690 --> 00:19:27.540
like a unikernel
that contains my app,

00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:29.370
and all the libraries
that it needs,

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:31.106
like DNS and all
that stuff, that it's

00:19:31.106 --> 00:19:32.700
going to need for web servers?

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:33.700
JOHN FEMINELLA: Mmm-hmm.

00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:35.700
FRANSESC CAMPOY: But it
could not include things

00:19:35.700 --> 00:19:38.430
like, I don't know, like USB-C
controller, or a floppy disk

00:19:38.430 --> 00:19:40.080
controller, or stuff like that?

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:41.180
Is that correct?

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:42.305
JOHN FEMINELLA: Yes, right.

00:19:44.810 --> 00:19:46.550
Now, normally the
container that,

00:19:46.550 --> 00:19:48.570
like-- like, if you're
running on Alpine Linux,

00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:52.560
it probably saves a lot
of space by omitting

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:54.480
a lot of those kinds
of firmware things

00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:56.850
that you aren't going to
need for a web application.

00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:58.300
So you're already
probably saving

00:19:58.300 --> 00:20:03.150
some space there by using just
a small base image for something

00:20:03.150 --> 00:20:04.830
like a Docker container.

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:08.700
But for your Go web application
example, what you'd save over

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:11.540
the Docker container
are all the parts--

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:14.240
you would literally be removing
everything from the kernel

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:16.740
that your application
doesn't actually depend on.

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:22.220
Like, when you use a base image
from someone else's Docker

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:25.970
layer, you're
basically saying, I'm

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:28.590
going to use that because
I know it's minimal,

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:32.060
but there's no guarantee from a
compiler or from something else

00:20:32.060 --> 00:20:34.570
that there isn't stuff in
there that you don't need.

00:20:34.570 --> 00:20:35.070
Right?

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:38.450
Like, you're still--
there's no tool that's

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:41.467
taking your application,
analyzing it in some way,

00:20:41.467 --> 00:20:43.800
and then saying, OK, here's
what you actually depend on,

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:48.060
here's the system calls you're
making, or whatever, and then,

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:49.420
compiling that unikernel.

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:51.610
So you have a sort
of a-- I think

00:20:51.610 --> 00:20:53.110
the goal is that,
effectively, you'd

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:57.550
have a stronger guarantee
about that thing that's

00:20:57.550 --> 00:21:01.880
the output of the compilation
process being minimal

00:21:01.880 --> 00:21:03.480
for some definition of minimal.

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:07.200
That is, it's smaller to run,
it's quicker to start up,

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:08.000
and so on.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:09.846
So that's what I would say.

00:21:09.846 --> 00:21:11.220
I think you're
essentially right.

00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:15.480
But I think there's the key
difference in that a computer

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:17.990
program is making a
decision about what's

00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:20.550
going into your
image, as opposed

00:21:20.550 --> 00:21:23.130
to a human being saying, I
want this, and I want that,

00:21:23.130 --> 00:21:24.520
and I want this other thing.

00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:26.920
That's sort of, I would say,
the fundamental distinction.

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:27.836
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:21:27.836 --> 00:21:30.610
So I could say
that it's basically

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:33.610
the difference between
having just a binary with all

00:21:33.610 --> 00:21:36.550
the possible symbols of all the
libraries that you are actually

00:21:36.550 --> 00:21:40.700
importing to actually stripping
the binary down to the objects

00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:43.310
that you're actually using--
that your program is actually

00:21:43.310 --> 00:21:44.120
using?

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:45.279
JOHN FEMINELLA: Right.

00:21:45.279 --> 00:21:47.070
I would say it goes
one step farther, which

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:49.420
is that your Go
binary, when you deploy

00:21:49.420 --> 00:21:54.790
that, you run that as a
process on an operating system.

00:21:54.790 --> 00:21:59.020
The equivalent thing on a
unikernel is that you run that,

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:01.270
and it is the operating system.

00:22:01.270 --> 00:22:04.970
The only thing it knows how
to do is run your web server.

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:06.850
It can't start up MySQL.

00:22:06.850 --> 00:22:09.470
It can't connect to
[INAUDIBLE], or whatever.

00:22:09.470 --> 00:22:13.790
The only thing it knows how
to do is run your process.

00:22:13.790 --> 00:22:16.190
It's a single consolidated
machine image.

00:22:16.190 --> 00:22:19.650
So it's like the kernel-- it
would be as though the Linux

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:22.680
kernel was explicitly
written to run your Go web

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:25.880
server and your Go
application, and nothing else.

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:27.840
So you're just
starting that thing,

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:31.200
and then you're making a million
copies of it or whatever.

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:35.105
FRANSESC CAMPOY: So it
could be like the Linux

00:22:35.105 --> 00:22:37.730
that I could start, it could be
distribution that only contains

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:42.370
my binary, and my binary could
be run as, like, process ID 0,

00:22:42.370 --> 00:22:43.010
or whatever.

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:43.160
JOHN FEMINELLA: Right.

00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:44.910
FRANSESC CAMPOY: But like,
it could be the main process.

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:45.710
Huh, interesting.

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:46.710
JOHN FEMINELLA: Exactly.

00:22:46.710 --> 00:22:49.280
It's kind of like, if you're
familiar with the privilege

00:22:49.280 --> 00:22:52.210
levels in traditional
kernels, you

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:55.650
may have heard of things like
Ring 0, Ring 1, Ring 2, Ring 3.

00:22:55.650 --> 00:23:00.065
So userland code in usually
executes as unprivileged.

00:23:00.065 --> 00:23:02.360
It lives in the top ring.

00:23:02.360 --> 00:23:06.570
And then, there are basically
context switches that

00:23:06.570 --> 00:23:08.070
happen into privileged mode.

00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:09.820
I mean, that's
how your kernel is

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:11.520
able to do things
with device drivers,

00:23:11.520 --> 00:23:14.080
and write to protected
areas of memory, and so on.

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:16.830
With a unikernel
operating system,

00:23:16.830 --> 00:23:20.660
your application slash
machine image is executing

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:21.910
everything in privileged mode.

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:23.980
Everything happens at Ring 0.

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:25.190
There's no contact switches.

00:23:25.190 --> 00:23:29.164
There's no handing back
and forth to userland code,

00:23:29.164 --> 00:23:30.580
because there is
no userland code.

00:23:30.580 --> 00:23:33.480
Everything is inside
the kernel itself.

00:23:33.480 --> 00:23:34.230
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:36.730
So does that mean that,
if I'm building myself

00:23:36.730 --> 00:23:39.450
a unikernel app, I need to
understand how, like, the Linux

00:23:39.450 --> 00:23:41.880
kernel works at a low
level, so that I can tell it

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:43.480
what drivers to put
in and pull out,

00:23:43.480 --> 00:23:44.772
or how is that process managed?

00:23:44.772 --> 00:23:45.646
JOHN FEMINELLA: Yeah.

00:23:45.646 --> 00:23:48.320
I mean, that's part of where
the usability efforts, I think,

00:23:48.320 --> 00:23:49.380
are coming in.

00:23:49.380 --> 00:23:51.320
So first of all, it's
not necessarily the case

00:23:51.320 --> 00:23:54.210
that you need, quote,
unquote, like "Linux kernel

00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:58.210
understanding," because there's
so much modification happening

00:23:58.210 --> 00:24:02.479
to these unikernel OS's
that some of them-- like,

00:24:02.479 --> 00:24:04.020
if you looked at
what the kernel code

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:06.060
looks like, it would be
unrecognizable compared

00:24:06.060 --> 00:24:08.950
to the Linux kernel code link.

00:24:08.950 --> 00:24:10.684
There's probably
some basic ideas

00:24:10.684 --> 00:24:12.600
that are going to be in
every modern operating

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:17.650
system, like memory
allocation and virtual memory,

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:18.610
or things like that.

00:24:18.610 --> 00:24:21.670
So those ideas will probably
have familiar implementations.

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:23.610
But in terms of, like,
looking at the code,

00:24:23.610 --> 00:24:26.637
and needing to understand
what some constant means,

00:24:26.637 --> 00:24:28.470
or something like that,
no, you don't really

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:30.100
have to do any of
that to get started.

00:24:30.100 --> 00:24:32.140
So like with Mirage
OS, for instance,

00:24:32.140 --> 00:24:36.900
you-- I will say
their documentation

00:24:36.900 --> 00:24:44.120
is very good-- but basically you
effectively just write an OCaml

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:47.840
application, and from your
application's perspective

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:49.290
nothing really looks different.

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:53.660
You can't tell that it's running
on a unikernel environment.

00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:55.570
You know, the Mirage
OS compiler is

00:24:55.570 --> 00:24:58.810
taking care of all the details
of how that application is

00:24:58.810 --> 00:25:02.430
actually doing
things, because it's

00:25:02.430 --> 00:25:07.340
sort of using its own stack,
its own kernel, its own OS,

00:25:07.340 --> 00:25:12.667
its own kernel OS, and
services on top of that OS,

00:25:12.667 --> 00:25:14.750
to make your application
work in this environment.

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:16.167
And that's great,
because it means

00:25:16.167 --> 00:25:18.500
that all the stuff that your
application doesn't rely on

00:25:18.500 --> 00:25:20.180
is not part of the
kernel to begin with,

00:25:20.180 --> 00:25:23.820
so you, again, save the
extra heavyweight steps,

00:25:23.820 --> 00:25:25.520
or extra heavyweight
bits, of the kernel

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:27.964
that you are not using.

00:25:27.964 --> 00:25:28.880
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:25:28.880 --> 00:25:31.620
So we're almost
running out of time,

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:35.690
but there is a topic that I
could not be happy leaving out,

00:25:35.690 --> 00:25:40.460
which is what about people that
say that unikernels are not

00:25:40.460 --> 00:25:42.006
fit for production?

00:25:42.006 --> 00:25:42.880
JOHN FEMINELLA: Sure.

00:25:42.880 --> 00:25:46.910
I mean, I think that that's
maybe a little premature, given

00:25:46.910 --> 00:25:51.900
that it's still a relatively new
area of not only development,

00:25:51.900 --> 00:25:54.880
but I think also
thought about philosophy

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:56.470
of how we're going to use this.

00:25:56.470 --> 00:26:01.290
And I think we don't want to be
too quick to set aside a tool,

00:26:01.290 --> 00:26:04.570
just because there may be some
valid objections to it to begin

00:26:04.570 --> 00:26:06.490
with.

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:09.190
You know, I think that
probably containers were not

00:26:09.190 --> 00:26:13.094
ready for production in 2013.

00:26:13.094 --> 00:26:15.510
Or I should say, Docker wasn't
really ready for production

00:26:15.510 --> 00:26:18.930
in 2013, and Solomon Hykes
and all the Docker people

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:22.240
vociferously said this multiple
times throughout 2013--

00:26:22.240 --> 00:26:23.736
that it's not ready
for production,

00:26:23.736 --> 00:26:26.110
but, you know, if you're brave
and you want to experiment

00:26:26.110 --> 00:26:27.821
with it, you certainly can.

00:26:27.821 --> 00:26:29.570
And I think that's
kind of the phase we're

00:26:29.570 --> 00:26:33.080
in now with unikernels, where
this is really just starting

00:26:33.080 --> 00:26:35.220
to catch the
attention of people.

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:37.830
And there are many
implementations.

00:26:37.830 --> 00:26:41.850
They're all trying to
demonstrate that their version

00:26:41.850 --> 00:26:43.580
is a good one.

00:26:43.580 --> 00:26:46.630
And we're still
waiting for the dust

00:26:46.630 --> 00:26:50.780
to settle a lot before we
start getting into these more

00:26:50.780 --> 00:26:53.236
specific details of, like,
how does debugging work

00:26:53.236 --> 00:26:54.360
in a unikernel environment?

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:57.350
How does tracing work?

00:26:57.350 --> 00:26:59.950
What's the right way to do that?

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:01.550
How do you deploy these things?

00:27:01.550 --> 00:27:03.730
Are the kinds of applications
that are suitable

00:27:03.730 --> 00:27:06.570
for unikernels maybe different
than what we're imagining,

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:08.690
or what we think
might be suitable?

00:27:08.690 --> 00:27:11.040
So there's a whole
range of, I think,

00:27:11.040 --> 00:27:13.390
questions we can ask
about unikernels that

00:27:13.390 --> 00:27:17.860
don't have answers right now,
or the community is working on,

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:19.550
trying to figure
out the answers for.

00:27:19.550 --> 00:27:21.380
So it's definitely
still early days,

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:28.680
and I certainly wouldn't be,
like, running my Fortune 100

00:27:28.680 --> 00:27:32.680
bank enterprise stuff on it.

00:27:32.680 --> 00:27:36.340
But I think it's absolutely
worth taking a look at,

00:27:36.340 --> 00:27:40.640
keeping an eye on, because
chances are good that, in 2018,

00:27:40.640 --> 00:27:43.400
when you guys have me
on for the two year

00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:48.290
anniversary of this
podcast, you can ask me,

00:27:48.290 --> 00:27:50.410
where have unikernels been
on the last two years,

00:27:50.410 --> 00:27:54.260
and I bet my answer will be
something like it probably took

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:55.740
the same path that
containers did,

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:57.360
where there was
gradual adoption.

00:27:57.360 --> 00:27:59.170
We realized that there
is a specialized use

00:27:59.170 --> 00:28:02.230
case for this
technology, and then

00:28:02.230 --> 00:28:04.880
we exploited that specialized
use case to really get

00:28:04.880 --> 00:28:06.990
the benefits of the technology.

00:28:06.990 --> 00:28:08.490
Just like containers
aren't suitable

00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:10.460
for all possible
applications, unikernels

00:28:10.460 --> 00:28:12.460
won't be suitable for all
possible applications.

00:28:12.460 --> 00:28:15.952
There will be some specific
set of things that's really

00:28:15.952 --> 00:28:18.410
well suited for unikernels and
some things that aren't, and

00:28:18.410 --> 00:28:19.504
that's OK.

00:28:19.504 --> 00:28:20.920
FRANSESC CAMPOY:
Very interesting.

00:28:20.920 --> 00:28:25.800
And you actually mentioned
Amir Chaudhry from Docker,

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:27.970
and you also mentioned Docker.

00:28:27.970 --> 00:28:31.580
So I got to meet him
during Craft Conf.

00:28:31.580 --> 00:28:33.880
We'll have a link to
the slides of his talk--

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:35.250
were really interesting.

00:28:35.250 --> 00:28:38.210
But I'd be curious to
know your opinion on why

00:28:38.210 --> 00:28:41.400
do you think Amir is now
working for Docker, meaning

00:28:41.400 --> 00:28:45.495
why did Docker buy that
unikernels company?

00:28:45.495 --> 00:28:46.370
JOHN FEMINELLA: Yeah.

00:28:46.370 --> 00:28:48.828
So first of all, I'll just say
that I've never actually met

00:28:48.828 --> 00:28:50.360
Amir in person,
but we've chatted

00:28:50.360 --> 00:28:51.790
a bunch over Google Hangout.

00:28:51.790 --> 00:28:54.130
So thanks, Google.

00:28:54.130 --> 00:28:58.637
But he is just clearly
doing amazing things,

00:28:58.637 --> 00:28:59.720
and is a brilliant person.

00:28:59.720 --> 00:29:05.560
And I think that, even if
the company had zero value,

00:29:05.560 --> 00:29:08.000
it would probably be worth
it to hire Amir and his team,

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:12.720
because there's a
certain set of people

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:14.350
that, if you talk
to them, you just

00:29:14.350 --> 00:29:15.530
realize that whatever
they're working on

00:29:15.530 --> 00:29:17.100
is probably going to be amazing.

00:29:17.100 --> 00:29:20.440
And so I don't want to set
Amir up for too much pressure,

00:29:20.440 --> 00:29:22.190
but I'm sure that
whatever he's working on

00:29:22.190 --> 00:29:25.230
is going to be great, and I
look forward to seeing it.

00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:27.550
So I think, if
Docker hired them,

00:29:27.550 --> 00:29:31.550
it's probably because they
saw what Amir and his team

00:29:31.550 --> 00:29:33.450
are capable of, and
what they were building,

00:29:33.450 --> 00:29:35.370
and they wanted a piece of that.

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:37.210
And I think it's
probably fair to say

00:29:37.210 --> 00:29:40.887
that, if Docker bought a company
called Unikernel Systems,

00:29:40.887 --> 00:29:42.720
and the word unikernel
is right in the name,

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:46.110
then they're certainly
exploring, and or actively

00:29:46.110 --> 00:29:49.360
using, unikernels
in a way that's

00:29:49.360 --> 00:29:52.880
probably ahead of the rest of
the industry at this point.

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:55.670
So I expect that, as we
start seeing industry

00:29:55.670 --> 00:29:58.860
best practices come out
of the operation of things

00:29:58.860 --> 00:30:03.241
like unikernels, I expect
to hear a lot about Docker

00:30:03.241 --> 00:30:05.240
and what they're doing
with it, and kind of look

00:30:05.240 --> 00:30:08.181
to them as a role model
for how we use unikernels.

00:30:08.181 --> 00:30:09.750
FRANSESC CAMPOY:
Well, I guess we

00:30:09.750 --> 00:30:12.100
have to wait till the second
anniversary of this podcast

00:30:12.100 --> 00:30:16.052
to interview you again and
see how much of that was true.

00:30:16.052 --> 00:30:17.510
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
go back in time.

00:30:17.510 --> 00:30:19.610
So we are running out of
time a little bit, John,

00:30:19.610 --> 00:30:22.511
but is there anything that you
would like to mention that you

00:30:22.511 --> 00:30:24.010
haven't had a chance
to mention yet,

00:30:24.010 --> 00:30:27.040
or a topic you want
to cover real quick?

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:30.377
JOHN FEMINELLA: Well, I would
say that, if folks-- or so,

00:30:30.377 --> 00:30:32.710
as I said, one of the things
that I'm very interested in

00:30:32.710 --> 00:30:40.090
is how businesses use platforms
in their deployment pipeline.

00:30:40.090 --> 00:30:43.350
Like, where do they see
platforms coming into play?

00:30:43.350 --> 00:30:48.580
And we've gradually been very
excited about containers.

00:30:48.580 --> 00:30:51.650
Then, people realized, OK, well
the orchestration of containers

00:30:51.650 --> 00:30:53.990
is a problem, so we have
things like Kubernetes,

00:30:53.990 --> 00:30:56.160
and other technologies
to help us,

00:30:56.160 --> 00:30:58.540
like, manage those containers.

00:30:58.540 --> 00:31:03.459
But I still feel like
there's something

00:31:03.459 --> 00:31:05.500
that we should be thinking
about, as an industry,

00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:08.220
in the workflow with
respect to if you

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:10.830
had a developer-- a
container, or a unikernel,

00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:13.040
or a virtual machine,
or something that's

00:31:13.040 --> 00:31:16.710
not their application--
then they're still

00:31:16.710 --> 00:31:19.810
responsible for a lot
of the stack, right?

00:31:19.810 --> 00:31:21.660
Like, they have to
do a lot more things

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:23.370
before it's actually running.

00:31:23.370 --> 00:31:26.610
And I want to see
us, as an industry,

00:31:26.610 --> 00:31:29.310
move towards better
tooling around

00:31:29.310 --> 00:31:33.970
how we manage containers,
and how we turn applications

00:31:33.970 --> 00:31:37.826
into it's running somewhere
and I can, like, you know,

00:31:37.826 --> 00:31:39.850
hit it with an HTTP request.

00:31:39.850 --> 00:31:43.000
And that, I think,
is an area that we

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:45.850
need to pay more attention to,
and that we are sort of slowly

00:31:45.850 --> 00:31:46.659
evolving.

00:31:46.659 --> 00:31:48.200
Platforms are playing
a role in that,

00:31:48.200 --> 00:31:50.616
but I know that we're going
to see awesome things come out

00:31:50.616 --> 00:31:52.550
of Kubernetes, and
other technologies.

00:31:52.550 --> 00:31:57.100
So I'm encouraging the
listeners of this podcast

00:31:57.100 --> 00:32:00.140
to think about, when you
deploy an application,

00:32:00.140 --> 00:32:03.070
is your next step, like,
put this in a container,

00:32:03.070 --> 00:32:06.709
or do you want this to be, hey,
do something that puts this

00:32:06.709 --> 00:32:08.500
in a container for me,
so then I don't have

00:32:08.500 --> 00:32:09.675
to worry about the details.

00:32:09.675 --> 00:32:12.050
Like, that, I think, is a
really interesting proposition,

00:32:12.050 --> 00:32:15.810
when I can get back to
writing code, instead of,

00:32:15.810 --> 00:32:19.670
like, operationally managing
37 containers and clustering

00:32:19.670 --> 00:32:22.160
technologies, and whatnot.

00:32:22.160 --> 00:32:23.310
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Very good.

00:32:23.310 --> 00:32:24.018
Very interesting.

00:32:24.018 --> 00:32:25.680
I totally agree
with that, actually.

